#const AL 32
#const AX 33

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

  AX
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

  AL
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    land_percent 15
    base_size 6
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_62 - is it the ID that makes a land, or the POSITION?
Feeds: docs/known-issues.md BUG-005 piece 2, CREATION_PLAN A.2
Run 61 first. This file only means something against 61's histogram.

WHAT 61 RETURNED
Three arms, read at Normal on 2026-08-12:

    create_land -> DIRT     6063     the control ran
    AL (32)     -> SNOW     absent
    AX (33)     -> DESERT   6233

Read literally that says `create_land` is token 33 on this build and 32 is
inert, which would mean the `#const L 32` idiom in `24hr_Petra.rms` and
`24hr_Holler.rms` does nothing in game. Those two maps contain NO other land
command - no `create_land`, no `create_player_lands` - so under that reading they
generate no lands at all, which is a strong claim to rest on one generation.

THE CONFOUND, AND IT IS MINE
61's two aliased arms differed in id AND in position. `AL` was the second land
command and `AX` was the third. So two models predict 61's exact histogram:

  A. The ID selects the command. 33 is create_land, 32 is not.
  B. POSITION decides. An unrecognised word leaves its block to be absorbed by
     whatever the engine is doing next, so the middle arm dies whatever its id
     is, and the last arm runs whatever its id is.

Nothing in 61 separates them, which is a design fault in 61 rather than a
surprise in the engine.

METHOD
Identical file, with the two aliased arms SWAPPED in both id and terrain. `AX`
(33) is now the middle arm and carries SNOW; `AL` (32) is now the last arm and
carries DESERT. The control is untouched and still first.

READ-OFF

  DIRT + SNOW, no DESERT
      Model A. The id is what selects the command and create_land is 33. The
      author's 32 is wrong on this build, BUG-005's 581 warnings are TRUE
      positives, and the alias table is written against 33.
  DIRT + DESERT, no SNOW
      Model B. Position decides, both 61 and this run are artefacts of block
      association, and nothing has been learned about any id. Stop; the
      mechanism needs a different instrument.
  DIRT + SNOW + DESERT
      Both aliases work, so 61's missing snow was something else entirely -
      most likely the middle-arm absorption of model B happening only in 61's
      ordering. Rerun 61 before reading further.
  DIRT only
      Neither alias works in either position. 32 and 33 are both inert and
      create_land is a third id.

IF MODEL A HOLDS, THE LIKELY STORY IS VERSION DRIFT, and it is worth saying
before anyone calls those maps sloppy. The equivalencies data is from the AoK/HD
era and both maps predate DE. One command inserted into the engine's token table
shifts every later id by one, which is exactly the 32-against-33 shape seen here.
It also predicts something checkable without any new script: generate
`24hr_Petra.rms` in DE and look for lands. No lands confirms the drift and turns
this into a finding about the maps rather than about our parser.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count, ONE RUN, same settings as 61. The
comparison is only valid against 61's own histogram, so do not change the size.
*/
